{
  "version": 1,
  "lang": "en",
  "cells": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "# Dreamy Researcher — code cell example\n\nThis notebook is a small end-to-end test:\n\n1. Python basics and persistent variables\n2. Creating a pandas DataFrame\n3. Summary statistics\n4. A small scikit-learn model\n5. Reusing variables from earlier cells\n6. Catching an error\n\nRun the cells top to bottom, or use **Run all**.\n"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "## 1. Python basics\n\nThe final expression in a code cell is displayed, similar to a notebook.\n"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "value": "# Basic Python — this cell defines variables used later\nname = \"researcher\"\nsquares = [n ** 2 for n in range(1, 11)]\n\nprint(f\"Hello, {name}!\")\nprint(\"Squares:\", squares)\nprint(\"Sum of squares:\", sum(squares))\n\nlen(squares)"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "## 2. Create a small dataset\n\nEverything below uses only NumPy and pandas.\n"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "value": "import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(42)\nn = 120\n\nstudy_hours = rng.normal(5, 1.5, n).clip(0, 12)\nsleep_hours = rng.normal(7, 1.0, n).clip(3, 10)\nattendance = rng.integers(60, 101, n)\n\nscore = (\n    6 * study_hours\n    + 2 * sleep_hours\n    + 0.25 * attendance\n    + rng.normal(0, 8, n)\n    - 5\n).clip(0, 100)\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"study_hours\": study_hours.round(2),\n    \"sleep_hours\": sleep_hours.round(2),\n    \"attendance\": attendance,\n    \"score\": score.round(2),\n    \"passed\": (score >= 60).astype(int),\n})\n\nprint(\"Shape:\", df.shape)\ndf.head()"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "## 3. Explore the data\n"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "value": "print(df.describe().round(2))\nprint(\"\\nMissing values:\")\nprint(df.isna().sum())\nprint(\"\\nPass rate:\", round(df[\"passed\"].mean() * 100, 1), \"%\")"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "## 4. Train a quick model\n\nThis uses `passed` as the target and the three numeric features.\n"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "value": "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\nfrom sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, classification_report\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n\nfeatures = [\"study_hours\", \"sleep_hours\", \"attendance\"]\nX = df[features]\ny = df[\"passed\"]\n\nX_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n    X, y, test_size=0.25, random_state=42, stratify=y\n)\n\nmodel = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=100, random_state=42)\nmodel.fit(X_train, y_train)\npredictions = model.predict(X_test)\n\nprint(\"Accuracy:\", round(accuracy_score(y_test, predictions), 3))\nprint(classification_report(y_test, predictions, target_names=[\"fail\", \"pass\"]))\n\npd.Series(model.feature_importances_, index=features).sort_values(ascending=False)"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "## 5. Check that Python state persists across cells\n\nThis cell uses `df`, `model`, and `features` from the previous cells.\n"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "value": "best_feature = features[model.feature_importances_.argmax()]\n\nprint(\"Rows in df:\", len(df))\nprint(\"Best feature:\", best_feature)\nprint(\"Best feature importance:\", round(float(model.feature_importances_.max()), 3))\n\n# Reuse a variable from the first cell\nprint(\"Hello again,\", name)"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "## 6. Error handling test\n\nThe app should print a readable error and keep the rest of the notebook usable.\n"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "value": "try:\n    1 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError as exc:\n    print(\"Caught the expected error:\", exc)\n\nprint(\"The notebook is still running.\")"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "value": "## Try the sidebar modules\n\nA CSV version of this dataset (without the intermediate `score` column) is\navailable as `samples/study-scores.csv`.\n\n1. Click **Load CSV…** and open `samples/study-scores.csv`.\n2. Run **Preprocessing**.\n3. Run **Visualization**.\n4. Choose **SHAP** or **LIME** and click **Explain**.\n\nYou can also export the generated data from a code cell:\n\n```python\ndf.to_csv(\"study-scores.csv\", index=False)\nprint(\"Wrote study-scores.csv\")\n```\n"
    }
  ]
}
